### Approach 1: Simple Solution using Sets (Swift)

In Swift, we can use a set to keep track of the inner lists we've seen so far. Since sets in Swift don't support lists as elements (because lists are not hashable), we'll convert the inner lists to tuples, which are hashable.

```swift
func removeDuplicates(_ lists: [[Int]]) -> [[Int]] {
    var seen = Set<String>()
    var result: [[Int]] = []

    for list in lists {
        let tuple = list.map { String($0) }.joined(separator: ",")
        if !seen.contains(tuple) {
            seen.insert(tuple)
            result.append(list)
        }
    }

    return result
}

